---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import "../../../examples/card.css";
import { cardCopy } from "../../../examples/card-data";
import { cardSources } from "../../../examples/card-sources";
import {
  CardAccentSoftDemo,
  CardAccentSolidDemo,
  CardActionDemo,
  CardBasicDemo,
  CardGradientDemo,
  CardLinkDemo,
  CardMediaDemo,
  CardMediaLinkDemo,
  CardMetaDemo,
  CardProductDemo,
  CardSelectDemo,
  CardStatDemo,
} from "../../../components/react-demos/card";

/* The twelve examples are a LADDER: each rung adds one decision to the one before it, and every
 * rung is shown as a grid of three, because a card that only looks right on its own is a card whose
 * height, media and footer were never tested against its siblings. */
const copy = cardCopy.en;
const src = cardSources(copy);
---

<Base
  title="Card"
  description="Card is not a component: Guide to composing semantic cards with Box, Tile, and content components."
>
  <h1>Card</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Card is a <strong>composition result of</strong>, not a system component. The content decides its structure; the interaction decides whether the surface is born from <a href="/en/components/box">Box</a> or from <a href="/en/components/tile">Tile</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
      <div class="sk-alert__description">
        There are no <code>sk-card</code>, <code>@skryensya/react/card</code>, or
        <code>components/card.css</code>. Import the pieces the card actually uses.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose by behavior</h2>
  <p>
    “Card” describes the visual form, but does not say what it does. Start with semantics and interaction; then compose the content with Stack, Inline, Heading, Text, Badge, Stat or other pieces.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The surface…</th><th>Use</th><th>Elemento real</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>presents content or contains multiple controls</td><td><a href="/en/components/box">Box</a></td><td><code>article</code>, <code>section</code> or <code>div</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>navigates to a destination completely</td><td><a href="/en/components/link">TileLink</a></td><td><code>a[href]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>executes an action completely</td><td><a href="/en/components/button">TileButton</a></td><td><code>button</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>activates a separate option</td><td><a href="/en/components/checkbox">TileCheckbox</a></td><td><code>input[type=checkbox]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>chooses a unique option</td><td><a href="/en/components/radio-group">TileRadioGroup</a></td><td><code>input[type=radio]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>reveals content</td><td><a href="/en/components/accordion">Accordion</a></td><td><code>button</code> or <code>details</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Box and Tile here start from the same surface, subtle edge, radius and padding <code>lg</code>. Tile preserves that content and adds the <code>sk-interactive</code> state layer; The visible difference appears when hovering, focusing or pressing, not in a second card recipe.
  </p>

  <h2 id="basic">Basic card</h2>
  <p>
    The floor of the ladder: a surface, a title, a paragraph. Nothing here is interactive, so the root is <a href="/en/components/box">Box</a> on an <code>article</code>. No <code>card</code> class is involved — only the surface, the subtle border and padding <code>lg</code>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.basic.html}
    react={src.basic.react}
    label={copy.labels.basic}
    note="Box + Stack + Heading + Text"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardBasicDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="meta">Status and date</h2>
  <p>
    The same Box, now with hierarchy: <a href="/en/components/badge">Badge</a> states the status and a Text at <code>caption</code> the date. The card did not grow an API, it grew content.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.meta.html}
    react={src.meta.react}
    label={copy.labels.meta}
    note="Box + Inline + Badge + Text"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardMetaDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="accent-soft">Soft accent</h2>
  <p>
    The accent background is asked for by rewriting the hooks Box already publishes — <code>--sk-box-bg</code> to <code>--color-bg-accent-subtle</code> and the border to <code>--color-border-accent</code> — never with a raw <code>background</code> on <code>.sk-box</code>, which would bypass the surface the pattern owns. The fill is subtle, so the text keeps using the normal tokens.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.accentSoft.html}
    react={src.accentSoft.react}
    label={copy.labels.accentSoft}
    note="Box + surface hooks"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardAccentSoftDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="accent-solid">Solid accent</h2>
  <p>
    Once the fill is the action color, the text is no longer read against the page but against that fill, which is why it comes from <code>--color-text-on-accent</code>. In dark mode the accent is a <em>light</em> blue and that token flips to near-black: a <code>data-tone</code> here would resolve against the wrong background and land unreadable in one of the two modes.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.accentSolid.html}
    react={src.accentSolid.react}
    label={copy.labels.accentSolid}
    note="Box + action color + on-accent text"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardAccentSolidDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="stat-card">Metric card</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/en/components/stat">Stat</a> supplies the metric and Box supplies the card. The collection fits a grid without Stat becoming a surface or growing a <code>card</code> variant in its API.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.stat.html}
    react={src.stat.react}
    label={copy.labels.stat}
    note="Box + Stat + Icon"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardStatDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="link">Card that navigates</h2>
  <p>
    First interactive rung. The whole surface leads to <strong>one</strong> destination, so the root is the <code>a[href]</code> itself: a TileLink. There is no CSS-stretched link and no <code>onClick</code> on a <code>div</code>, and the title is what names the link.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.link.html}
    react={src.link.react}
    label={copy.labels.link}
    note="TileLink + Icon"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardLinkDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="action">Card that acts</h2>
  <p>
    Same geometry, different platform element: this one <em>does</em> something, so it is a <code>button</code>. The shape does not decide the element; the intent does.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.action.html}
    react={src.action.react}
    label={copy.labels.action}
    note="TileButton + Icon"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardActionDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="selection">Card that is chosen</h2>
  <p>
    And a preference is a checkbox. The surface is the same again, but the state belongs to the platform: it toggles with the space bar, it submits with a form, and a screen reader announces it as a checkbox.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.select.html}
    react={src.select.react}
    label={copy.labels.select}
    note="TileCheckbox"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardSelectDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="image">Card with image</h2>
  <p>
    Media arrives. The Box carries no padding: it already clips through <code>overflow</code>, so an <a href="/en/components/image-frame">ImageFrame</a> at <code>data-radius="none"</code> reaches the edge and inherits the rounded corner. The text inset is restored by <code>.sk-card-body</code>, because padding on the root would have inset the photo too.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.media.html}
    react={src.media.react}
    label={copy.labels.media}
    note="Box + ImageFrame + Badge"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardMediaDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="gradient">Card with gradient</h2>
  <p>
    The wash that keeps text readable over a photo. It sizes itself to the <strong>type</strong> it protects and not to a percentage of the image: the caption is the box and the gradient fills it, fading toward the photo. The three cards show the three <code>data-strength</code> values — <code>sm</code>, <code>md</code> and <code>lg</code> — over the same pale panel, because strength is opacity and tint and never how much of the image is covered. The images are deliberately light: over an already-dark background the wash would be invisible and the example would argue against itself.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.gradient.html}
    react={src.gradient.react}
    label={copy.labels.gradient}
    note="ImageFrame + MediaCaption + MediaGradient (sm · md · lg)"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardGradientDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="image-link">Card with image that navigates</h2>
  <p>
    All three at once: media, gradient and navigation. It is a TileLink at <code>data-padding="none"</code> so the photo reaches the edge, and everything inside is a <code>span</code>, because an <code>a</code> may not contain block-level interactive content.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.mediaLink.html}
    react={src.mediaLink.react}
    label={copy.labels.mediaLink}
    note="TileLink + ImageFrame + MediaGradient"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardMediaLinkDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2 id="product">Product card</h2>
  <p>
    The top of the ladder, and the example that proves the rule. <strong>Two</strong> independent decisions live here — see details and add — so the root cannot be a Tile: a link and a button nested inside an <code>a</code> is invalid HTML, and a whole-surface click could only ever mean one of the two. This is what Box is for.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview
    html={src.product.html}
    react={src.product.react}
    label={copy.labels.product}
    note="Box + Badge + Link + Button"
  >
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <CardProductDemo client:load lang="en" />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2>Do and don't</h2>
  <div class="sk-grid" data-columns="2" data-gap="md" data-multicol>
    <section class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
      <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
        <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="success">Do · Haz</span>
        <h3 class="sk-heading" data-size="h4">Let the behavior choose the root</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Use Box for content and for several independent controls.</li>
          <li>Use a semantic Tile when the entire surface has a single intent.</li>
          <li>Retains headings, lists, prices, and statuses as content components.</li>
          <li>Make the visible focus reach the entire interactive surface.</li>
          <li>Let the content determine the height; groups only comparable cards.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
      <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
        <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="danger">Do not Avoid</span>
        <h3 class="sk-heading" data-size="h4">Do not convert appearance into an API</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Do not create a Card component with <code>news</code>, <code>product</code>, or <code>stat</code> variants.</li>
          <li>Do not put <code>onClick</code> or <code>tabindex</code> in a Box or <code>div</code>.</li>
          <li>Do not nest buttons, links or inputs inside TileLink or TileButton.</li>
          <li>Do not duplicate the same destination on the surface and on an interior link.</li>
          <li>Do not cut important content just to match heights.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Implementation</h2>
  <p>
    There is no Card import. Import Box or the chosen semantic Tile, its styles and only the content pieces present. This keeps every dependency and every contract visible on the call site.
  </p>
</Base>
